WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.137
The first mistake in time management is assuming that a time block

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:06.491
is useful just because it is empty.

00:00:06.491 --> 00:00:11.200
In reality, attention and energy determine what kind of work fits inside the block.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.901
A half hour between meetings may be fine for administrative cleanup,

00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:19.274
but poor for architectural thinking, writing, code review, study, or a sensitive conversation.

00:00:19.274 --> 00:00:22.974
The same number of minutes can have very different productive capacity.

00:00:22.974 --> 00:00:25.665
Deep work requires sustained attention, a stable context,

00:00:25.665 --> 00:00:29.029
and enough cognitive room to hold the problem in mind.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.720
Shallow work is not bad; messages, approvals, scheduling,

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.074
and routine updates often keep operations moving.

00:00:34.074 --> 00:00:37.774
But shallow work becomes expensive when it constantly interrupts deep work.

00:00:37.774 --> 00:00:41.474
Each context switch forces attention to reload the task, reconstruct decisions,

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:43.493
and recover the thread of thought.

00:00:43.493 --> 00:00:46.857
Cognitive load is the mental demand placed on working memory.

00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:50.557
Fatigue, ambiguity, noise, interruptions, and emotional pressure all raise that load.

00:00:50.557 --> 00:00:53.584
When load is high, people may still push through,

00:00:53.584 --> 00:00:55.602
but accuracy and judgment often suffer.

00:00:55.602 --> 00:00:58.630
A time problem may actually be an attention problem.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.984
It may also be an energy problem,

00:01:00.984 --> 00:01:06.030
where the person has hours available but not enough recovery to use those hours well.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.057
A practical system matches task type to available state.

00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:10.739
High-consequence work deserves protected attention.

00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:13.430
Routine work can be grouped into lower-energy periods.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.458
Recovery should be treated as an input to performance,

00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:19.149
not as a reward for having already performed.

00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:21.503
When planning accounts for attention and energy,

00:01:21.503 --> 00:01:25.540
the calendar becomes more realistic and less likely to create false promises.
